  "headline": "Nine killed and 40 injured after gunmen open fire at church funeral in Jordan",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Nine people were killed and 40 others were injured after gunmen opened fire during a funeral at St George’s Greek Orthodox church in Hamoud, located in the Karak governorate of Jordan, on October 23, 2026. Authorities described the attack on the country’s Christian minority as a terrorist act carried out by extremists. While Interior Minister Mazin Al Farrayeh stated the attack was performed by extremists, he noted it was too early to determine if they had links to known groups inside or outside Jordan. There was no immediate claim of responsibility for the shooting. The church is situated approximately 90 kilometers (55 miles) south of the capital, Amman. Jordan’s Prime Minister, Jafar Hassan, characterized the event as a result of terrorist ideology and blind extremism. Maj. Gen. Abdallah al-Maaytah, head of Jordan’s Public Security Directorate, stated that the hunt for the attackers was continuing and that most of those injured were receiving treatment."
    },
    {
      "text": "Footage broadcast on Jordan’s state-owned Al-Mamlaka television station, which was verified by Reuters, showed three people firing at the church from a pavement and hiding behind trees and a low wall before running toward the entrance. While state TV footage depicted three attackers, a witness reported hearing that other people saw four gunmen; the Associated Press noted it could not independently verify the total number of assailants. One mourner, Dorgham Halasa, stated that intense gunfire erupted 15 minutes after the funeral began. A priest at the scene described ordering people to get to the ground as the shooting occurred. Witness video showed broken glass and pools of blood on the church floor. A government statement confirmed that investigations are underway, though it did not specify if any gunmen had been captured or killed."
    }
  ]
}

