  "headline": "Bangladesh Home Minister says next national election will be held under caretaker government",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Bangladesh Home Minister Salahuddin Ahmed stated on October 25, 2026, that the next national election will be held under a caretaker government. Speaking at a press briefing at the home ministry, Ahmed said a political consensus has been reached on the issue and that the system would be formally restored through a constitutional amendment. He noted that the Supreme Court had judicially revived the caretaker government system by overturning an earlier judgment by Justice Khairul Haque regarding the 13th amendment, though a formal amendment remains necessary for restoration. Ahmed described the restoration of the system as a commitment and a demand of their movement."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Albertans approve immigration questions but reject constitutional reforms in referendum",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "In a referendum in Alberta, Canada, voters approved five immigration-related questions but rejected constitutional reforms, according to results from the United Conservative government's nine-question ballot. Elections Alberta reported that the count, which involved hand-counting 20,574,612 ballots, was completed on the night of October 24, 2026. The agency noted this was approximately three days later than the intended deadline set under provincial referendum regulations. Elections Alberta stated this was the highest number of ballots ever hand-counted in a Canadian electoral event. Official results, including total voter turnout, are scheduled to be made official in October. 1. The question requiring proof of citizenship to vote in provincial elections received 58 per cent support. 2. Approximately 55 per cent of voters supported a pitch for the province to take greater control of immigration to decrease the number of newcomers. 3. Three questions regarding methods for limiting public and social services to certain non-permanent residents received 52, 52.6, and 53 per cent support, respectively. While results were still being processed on October 21, 2026, Alberta Premier Danielle Smith told reporters she would prioritize adjusting the province's approach to economic migrants but would proceed with caution on other immigration matters. Smith stated she wanted to be cautious because changing rules midstream can be difficult for those who arrived with certain expectations. Lori Williams, a political scientist at Mount Royal University, noted a rural-urban divide regarding the nine questions and expressed doubt that the results represented the strong mandate the government sought."
    }
  ]
}

